1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,660
Here is a simple tensile test: a machine
pulls a metal bar, and we

2
00:00:04,729 --> 00:00:06,289
measure how much it stretches.

3
00:00:06,722 --> 00:00:09,834
We want to know which of two models predicts
that elongation better.

4
00:00:10,335 --> 00:00:14,529
A simple regression model and a neural model
receive the same loading

5
00:00:14,575 --> 00:00:16,660
information and predict the same elongation.

6
00:00:17,162 --> 00:00:19,521
The neural model has a smaller average
error.

7
00:00:20,113 --> 00:00:21,868
Should we replace the simpler model?

8
00:00:22,187 --> 00:00:26,335
I'm Ricardo Fitas. We will answer that
question using a small paired

9
00:00:26,381 --> 00:00:30,779
comparison in Python. The application is a
concrete motivation; the five

10
00:00:30,801 --> 00:00:34,859
scores in this lesson are synthetic teaching
data, not measured results

11
00:00:34,893 --> 00:00:39,861
from either model. We will open Python
scripts in JupyterLab, execute

12
00:00:39,885 --> 00:00:42,335
the calculations, and watch the plots
develop.

13
00:00:42,836 --> 00:00:46,915
The workflow is to define the target,
protect the test set, compare the

14
00:00:46,962 --> 00:00:50,893
same cases, quantify the effect, and
describe the uncertainty that

15
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:52,351
remains.

16
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,481
Start with the intended use. Are you
predicting an unseen frame from a

17
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:02,439
familiar specimen, an entirely new specimen,
or an unseen operating regime?

18
00:01:03,136 --> 00:01:06,526
Those are different claims and usually
require different splits.

19
00:01:07,129 --> 00:01:10,740
Decide which error matters and what
improvement would be useful before

20
00:01:10,798 --> 00:01:15,117
examining the results. A small mean
reduction may have little value if the

21
00:01:15,175 --> 00:01:16,882
worst cases become unacceptable.

22
00:01:17,694 --> 00:01:21,978
Conversely, a modest change may matter if it
avoids a costly failure in the

23
00:01:22,048 --> 00:01:23,662
operating region you care about.

24
00:01:24,358 --> 00:01:28,608
State the baseline, the candidate, the unit
of evaluation and the

25
00:01:28,689 --> 00:01:30,210
information available to both.

26
00:01:30,906 --> 00:01:34,088
Record the model revision and the dataset
revision.

27
00:01:34,691 --> 00:01:38,581
This contract makes the conclusion
interpretable and reduces the temptation

28
00:01:38,627 --> 00:01:42,180
to change the question after seeing a
favourable number.

29
00:01:42,792 --> 00:01:45,811
Suppose each specimen produces one hundred
frames.

30
00:01:46,414 --> 00:01:49,456
You now have five hundred rows, but only
five specimens.

31
00:01:50,269 --> 00:01:54,658
Frames from the same tensile-test sequence
share geometry, preparation,

32
00:01:54,727 --> 00:01:59,000
loading and imaging conditions. Treating
every frame as an independent

33
00:01:59,035 --> 00:02:01,752
specimen overstates how much new evidence
you have.

34
00:02:02,564 --> 00:02:06,906
Independence is an assumption about how data
arise; it is not a property

35
00:02:06,964 --> 00:02:09,263
created by converting them into separate
rows.

36
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:13,570
For this worked example, we treat the five
hypothetical specimens as

37
00:02:13,652 --> 00:02:18,307
independent units. Within each specimen, the
frame residuals share a

38
00:02:18,365 --> 00:02:22,196
prescribed pattern. We will aggregate frame
errors into one score per

39
00:02:22,254 --> 00:02:24,925
specimen, then compare the models at that
level.

40
00:02:25,528 --> 00:02:29,325
This addresses a specimen-level question.
Other designs may require

41
00:02:29,371 --> 00:02:32,993
subjects, sites, sessions, batches or
contiguous time blocks as the

42
00:02:33,075 --> 00:02:34,665
evaluation unit.

43
00:02:35,292 --> 00:02:37,660
The same grouping should inform the split.

44
00:02:38,357 --> 00:02:41,817
If the deployment target is a new specimen,
putting frames from that

45
00:02:41,863 --> 00:02:45,706
specimen in both training and testing can
leak specimen-specific

46
00:02:45,764 --> 00:02:49,166
information. Keep related observations
together.

47
00:02:49,677 --> 00:02:53,879
Fit preprocessing using training data,
select hyperparameters using

48
00:02:53,926 --> 00:02:57,653
validation data, and reserve the final
evaluation for the claim you

49
00:02:57,699 --> 00:03:02,924
specified. If you use cross-validation for
model selection, the outer

50
00:03:02,993 --> 00:03:06,209
evaluation must remain separate from that
selection process.

51
00:03:06,906 --> 00:03:11,747
Also be cautious about uncertainty from
folds: folds can share most of their

52
00:03:11,805 --> 00:03:15,358
training data, so their scores are not
automatically independent

53
00:03:15,404 --> 00:03:20,397
experiments. Our example begins with fixed
hypothetical test-case scores.

54
00:03:20,907 --> 00:03:24,820
We are not training a neural network here,
and we will not imply that five

55
00:03:24,843 --> 00:03:27,502
scores demonstrate a completed nested
evaluation.

56
00:03:28,106 --> 00:03:32,262
The lesson is to preserve the information
boundary before computing

57
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:33,714
uncertainty.

58
00:03:34,333 --> 00:03:39,060
In JupyterLab, double-click the paired
scores script in the file browser.

59
00:03:39,571 --> 00:03:42,787
Open a terminal and enter python followed by
the script name.

60
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:48,383
Press Enter to run it. Open the generated
case table, with one row

61
00:03:48,987 --> 00:03:54,281
per hypothetical specimen. The baseline
errors are 0.11

62
00:03:54,304 --> 00:03:59,691
zero, 0.170, 0.140, 0.1

63
00:03:59,738 --> 00:04:01,967
nine zero and 0.130.

64
00:04:02,780 --> 00:04:08,155
The candidate errors are 0.095, 0.15

65
00:04:08,190 --> 00:04:13,380
zero, 0.125, 0.175 and zero point

66
00:04:13,461 --> 00:04:17,211
one two zero. Both columns describe the same
five cases.

67
00:04:17,652 --> 00:04:19,986
Return to the script and inspect the
subtraction.

68
00:04:20,590 --> 00:04:24,630
Candidate minus baseline is negative when
the candidate has lower error.

69
00:04:25,071 --> 00:04:29,634
Case one gives 0.095 minus 0.110,

70
00:04:29,843 --> 00:04:32,107
which is minus 0.015.

71
00:04:32,548 --> 00:04:35,555
Case two gives minus 0.020.

72
00:04:36,066 --> 00:04:40,245
Keep the identifiers beside both scores so
that sorting one column cannot

73
00:04:40,315 --> 00:04:43,798
silently break the pairing. The same script
calculates the means.

74
00:04:44,239 --> 00:04:48,210
The mean baseline score is 0.148, the mean
candidate

75
00:04:48,233 --> 00:04:52,598
score is 0.133, and the mean difference is
minus zero

76
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:57,196
point zero one five. The plot connects each
baseline score to its matching

77
00:04:57,231 --> 00:05:01,364
candidate score. Every line slopes down in
this constructed example.

78
00:05:01,968 --> 00:05:07,100
NumPy computes one difference per case; the
mean is a separate operation

79
00:05:07,193 --> 00:05:11,964
over those five differences. The worked
example also reconstructs frame

80
00:05:11,999 --> 00:05:16,573
residuals with exactly these case-level
root-mean-square errors and checks

81
00:05:16,608 --> 00:05:21,032
the result. Those checks connect the case
table to the dependent frame

82
00:05:21,067 --> 00:05:25,177
data. You can pause the video to calculate a
row yourself.

83
00:05:25,792 --> 00:05:29,020
We are calculating the mean of five case-
level RMSEs.

84
00:05:29,403 --> 00:05:32,944
That is not generally the same as pooling
every residual and calculating one

85
00:05:32,967 --> 00:05:38,830
global RMSE. With unequal sequence lengths,
a pooled metric can give longer

86
00:05:38,900 --> 00:05:42,696
sequences more weight. Different weighting
schemes answer different

87
00:05:42,731 --> 00:05:46,760
questions. Neither is automatically correct
for every deployment.

88
00:05:47,363 --> 00:05:52,251
Our equal-case average asks how the model
performs for a typical case within

89
00:05:52,286 --> 00:05:57,847
this fixed teaching set. The mean paired
difference of minus 0.015 is the

90
00:05:57,905 --> 00:05:59,403
effect estimate on that scale.

91
00:06:00,007 --> 00:06:04,442
The five lines on the plot show the per-case
changes, which the average

92
00:06:04,476 --> 00:06:08,911
alone would hide. In this constructed
example every line improves.

93
00:06:09,352 --> 00:06:13,869
With real data, inspect both helpful and
harmful cases and relate their

94
00:06:13,915 --> 00:06:17,247
sizes to an engineering or practical
threshold.

95
00:06:17,875 --> 00:06:21,602
Open the paired bootstrap script. To explore
variation in the average

96
00:06:21,625 --> 00:06:25,398
across cases, we resample case indices with
replacement.

97
00:06:25,909 --> 00:06:30,344
When case three is selected, we take both
its baseline and candidate score.

98
00:06:31,157 --> 00:06:33,073
Watch the paired rows move together.

99
00:06:33,247 --> 00:06:36,602
Resampling the two model arrays separately
would destroy that pairing.

100
00:06:37,415 --> 00:06:40,631
Look at the SciPy bootstrap call in the
editor.

101
00:06:41,234 --> 00:06:46,111
The arguments specify paired equals True,
the percentile method, a fixed

102
00:06:46,238 --> 00:06:48,804
random generator, and twenty thousand
resamples.

103
00:06:49,315 --> 00:06:53,169
Run the script in the terminal, then open
the generated animation.

104
00:06:53,495 --> 00:06:57,848
The statistic accepts an axis argument
because SciPy evaluates a batch of

105
00:06:57,930 --> 00:07:02,504
resamples. Taking the mean along that axis
preserves the batch dimension.

106
00:07:03,108 --> 00:07:07,055
In the output, the histogram accumulates
resampled mean differences.

107
00:07:07,566 --> 00:07:12,071
For these constructed inputs, the
illustrative interval runs from minus

108
00:07:12,117 --> 00:07:16,332
0.018 to minus 0.012.

109
00:07:16,715 --> 00:07:20,732
This is a distribution of resampled mean
differences, not individual frame

110
00:07:20,778 --> 00:07:25,457
residuals or model parameters. The animation
reveals the computed resamples

111
00:07:25,492 --> 00:07:28,116
in batches so that you can see the
calculation.

112
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:32,237
The fixed random generator makes this
particular run repeatable.

113
00:07:32,841 --> 00:07:36,440
Twenty thousand is a computational choice,
not the number of physical

114
00:07:36,498 --> 00:07:37,520
experiments.

115
00:07:38,125 --> 00:07:43,036
Watch the same bootstrap calculation after
one hundred, one thousand, five

116
00:07:43,071 --> 00:07:47,738
thousand and twenty thousand resamples. The
histogram and interval

117
00:07:47,808 --> 00:07:51,651
estimates become numerically more stable,
but the number of independent

118
00:07:51,709 --> 00:07:56,597
cases remains five. More resampling cannot
discover unseen specimen types,

119
00:07:56,771 --> 00:07:59,453
operating regimes or shared systematic
errors.

120
00:08:00,149 --> 00:08:04,956
The interval below zero therefore does not
automatically validate the

121
00:08:04,991 --> 00:08:09,298
candidate. For this tiny sample, its
numerical endpoints do not certify

122
00:08:09,379 --> 00:08:14,186
reliable population coverage. Now open the
separate central-limit animation

123
00:08:14,244 --> 00:08:18,702
in the file browser. Here we sample from a
known uniform population and

124
00:08:18,772 --> 00:08:21,837
increase the number of independent
observations in each average.

125
00:08:22,650 --> 00:08:26,214
The standardised distribution of sample
means approaches a Gaussian shape.

126
00:08:26,818 --> 00:08:30,951
That is a different experiment. Increasing
independent sample size is not

127
00:08:30,986 --> 00:08:34,376
the same as increasing the number of
bootstrap repeats on the same five

128
00:08:34,411 --> 00:08:39,647
cases. Our bootstrap distribution has
discrete support; we do not impose a

129
00:08:39,693 --> 00:08:43,699
Gaussian curve on it. A useful report
distinguishes computational

130
00:08:43,769 --> 00:08:48,134
approximation, limited independent evidence,
and a change in the population

131
00:08:48,226 --> 00:08:49,922
or operating conditions.

132
00:08:50,542 --> 00:08:55,488
Open the exact sign-flip script. Under a
null model that permits swapping

133
00:08:55,534 --> 00:08:59,528
the two model labels within each independent
case, how unusual is the

134
00:08:59,586 --> 00:09:03,708
observed mean difference? Each of five pairs
has two possible label

135
00:09:03,766 --> 00:09:07,679
assignments, giving two to the power five,
or thirty-two, assignments.

136
00:09:08,375 --> 00:09:12,160
Swapping labels changes the sign of one
case's difference while preserving

137
00:09:12,195 --> 00:09:15,992
the pair. Run the script in the terminal and
open its animation.

138
00:09:16,503 --> 00:09:20,311
The rows show every assignment of minus one
and plus one.

139
00:09:20,915 --> 00:09:23,213
Each column still belongs to one case.

140
00:09:23,655 --> 00:09:26,151
The next line computes a mean for every row.

141
00:09:26,754 --> 00:09:30,887
As the thirty-two outcomes appear, the
histogram builds the exact null

142
00:09:30,934 --> 00:09:35,404
distribution. The same script runs SciPy's
permutation test with

143
00:09:35,450 --> 00:09:40,338
permutation type samples, a two-sided
alternative, and infinite resamples.

144
00:09:40,942 --> 00:09:45,412
For this small example, infinity requests
exhaustive enumeration, not a

145
00:09:45,446 --> 00:09:50,416
Monte Carlo approximation. The assertions
confirm thirty-two assignments

146
00:09:50,451 --> 00:09:52,552
and agreement with the explicit enumeration.

147
00:09:53,063 --> 00:09:57,428
The exact two-sided p-value is 0.0625.

148
00:09:58,241 --> 00:10:01,515
That interpretation requires model-label
exchangeability under the

149
00:10:01,538 --> 00:10:06,368
specified null; a paired table alone does
not establish that assumption for

150
00:10:06,438 --> 00:10:10,513
every observational comparison. The code
checks the calculation.

151
00:10:10,954 --> 00:10:13,902
The experimental design must justify its
use.

152
00:10:14,542 --> 00:10:18,814
The percentile interval is below zero, while
the exact two-sided test

153
00:10:19,140 --> 00:10:21,636
does not cross a conventional five-percent
threshold.

154
00:10:22,448 --> 00:10:24,561
This is not a contradiction in the code.

155
00:10:25,072 --> 00:10:28,671
The methods use different constructions, and
small samples make their

156
00:10:28,741 --> 00:10:34,313
limitations visible. In this particular
five-pair sign-flip design, even

157
00:10:34,348 --> 00:10:38,180
improvements in every case give a smallest
attainable two-sided value of

158
00:10:38,272 --> 00:10:43,044
0.0625 when the nonzero differences have the
same sign.

159
00:10:43,857 --> 00:10:46,144
We verified that calculation by enumeration.

160
00:10:47,096 --> 00:10:50,835
Do not generalise that number to every
statistical test or every dataset

161
00:10:50,881 --> 00:10:55,525
with five observations. Also, a p-value is
not the probability that the

162
00:10:55,571 --> 00:10:59,821
candidate is better. It measures
compatibility with a specified null

163
00:10:59,879 --> 00:11:02,747
calculation, conditional on its assumptions.

164
00:11:03,444 --> 00:11:07,147
Effect size, uncertainty and the practical
decision still need to be

165
00:11:07,182 --> 00:11:08,180
discussed.

166
00:11:08,792 --> 00:11:10,371
Return to the residual arrays.

167
00:11:10,812 --> 00:11:13,227
Each has shape five by one hundred.

168
00:11:13,831 --> 00:11:16,675
We repeat the frames ten times and obtain
more columns.

169
00:11:17,279 --> 00:11:21,992
The per-case RMSE stays unchanged, and the
number of cases stays five.

170
00:11:22,689 --> 00:11:25,824
This executed check makes the distinction
concrete.

171
00:11:26,265 --> 00:11:29,945
Repeated or highly correlated measurements
can improve how a particular

172
00:11:30,015 --> 00:11:33,463
case is described, but they do not
automatically provide more

173
00:11:33,510 --> 00:11:37,898
independent cases for generalisation. A
frame-level analysis can be

174
00:11:37,933 --> 00:11:41,706
appropriate for a frame-level target if its
dependence is handled

175
00:11:41,741 --> 00:11:46,106
properly. The mistake is to use the number
of rows as the sample size

176
00:11:46,141 --> 00:11:47,627
for a different population claim.

177
00:11:48,231 --> 00:11:52,283
Before choosing a confidence procedure, draw
the data hierarchy and

178
00:11:52,329 --> 00:11:55,232
decide which unit the conclusion is about.

179
00:11:55,833 --> 00:11:59,049
A comparison also depends on which baselines
are included.

180
00:11:59,653 --> 00:12:03,089
A neural model should compete with simpler
approaches that receive

181
00:12:03,136 --> 00:12:05,736
comparable information and are tuned fairly.

182
00:12:06,433 --> 00:12:10,392
If you try many candidates and report only
the best final test score,

183
00:12:10,903 --> 00:12:13,376
the test set has become part of selection.

184
00:12:13,979 --> 00:12:18,101
Keep a record of the search and use a
protected evaluation or an

185
00:12:18,147 --> 00:12:22,698
appropriate nested design. Retain adverse
cases, failed fits and

186
00:12:22,780 --> 00:12:27,911
predefined exclusion rules. If random
initialisation matters, report it

187
00:12:27,969 --> 00:12:32,172
separately from variation across specimens:
many seeds on the same

188
00:12:32,195 --> 00:12:34,935
specimen are not many new specimens.

189
00:12:35,446 --> 00:12:39,824
For scientific models, check whether lower
observation error comes from

190
00:12:39,905 --> 00:12:43,608
recovering the intended mechanism or
compensating for missing physics.

191
00:12:44,212 --> 00:12:47,858
Better fit and more credible interpretation
are related but distinct

192
00:12:47,916 --> 00:12:49,286
objectives.

193
00:12:49,917 --> 00:12:53,864
For a practical decision, combine the effect
with the cost of obtaining

194
00:12:53,922 --> 00:12:58,265
it. You may care about accuracy in a
critical regime, robustness,

195
00:12:58,590 --> 00:13:02,305
inference latency, memory, training cost or
energy.

196
00:13:02,909 --> 00:13:06,926
Those claims need their own measurements and
a fair protocol; none is

197
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:08,946
supplied by our synthetic error table.

198
00:13:09,642 --> 00:13:13,230
A decision threshold should come from the
use case rather than from a

199
00:13:13,288 --> 00:13:17,758
convenient p-value. You can prefer a model
on engineering grounds while

200
00:13:17,781 --> 00:13:21,171
stating that evidence for wider
generalisation remains limited.

201
00:13:21,984 --> 00:13:25,061
You can also decide to collect more
independent cases before changing a

202
00:13:25,107 --> 00:13:29,926
safety-critical workflow. The report should
say what was compared, what

203
00:13:29,972 --> 00:13:33,896
information was available, what changed on
each case, and what would

204
00:13:33,978 --> 00:13:35,893
make you revise the conclusion.

205
